                <pb facs="0741" n="591" sameAs="frus:frus1907p2/pg591"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center"
                    >[Subinclosure.—Telegram.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Ramon Chan to
                        Minister Combs.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mazatenango</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1906-08-02"><hi rendition="#italic">August 2,
                        1906</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Desperate on account of the procedure of the parents of my wife, having exploited
                    me iniquitously and tired of their injuries, I committed a fault for which I
                    think I was excessively punished by the judge of peace of San Antonio, with
                    forty days’ imprisonment, commutable at $5 per day. Being occupied in my
                    business, the judge of first instancia ordered my capture, bringing me yesterday
                    to this capital of province to be newly tried for the same cause, what I
                    consider unjust. I earnestly beg of you to interpose your good offices with the
                    President of the Republic, so that they will set me at liberty, though it may be
                    under bail.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Ramon Chan.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
